[[b]]Absorb playbook[[/b]][[g]] — an existing codebase becomes specs; agent-executed (ADR 0005).[[/g]]

The intake holds the code INVENTORY, not a description. Describe what the
system does TODAY, as the code shows it — never what someone may want it
to do. Execute in order, in a single linear pass:

1. Read [[c]].doctrina/intake.md[[/c]] (the inventory), then what it points at:
   the README, the documentation and decisions it lists, the agent
   instructions already written, and per UNIT its manifest, its entry
   points, one or two files per candidate area and its tests. Breadth
   first — do not read the whole repository. Candidate areas are a hint
   from the folder layout, not capabilities.

2. Fill [[c]].doctrina/product.md[[/c]] with what is observable: what the system
   is, who uses it, what it does. Every business fact the code cannot tell
   you (why a rule exists, who the users are, what is out of scope) goes
   under [[c]]## Open questions[[/c]] for the human — never guess intent. So does
   every line of the inventory's "What this inventory could not see".

3. One capability per area of behaviour you READ. In a repository of
   several units, a capability stays inside one unit unless the code
   itself shares it (a library both import). For each:
       [[c]]doctrina spec new <capability>[[/c]]
   then write EARS requirements for the behaviour the code shows, with
   [[c]]**Implementation:** implemented[[/c]] and [[c]]**Source:**[[/c]] naming the real
   folders it owns (ADR 0027; `validate` refuses a Source that matches no
   file). Keep it a BASELINE: a few requirements per area now; the specs
   grow with every change that touches them.

4. No requirement without the code that does it:
   - Write behaviour only from code or a test you read — never from a
     folder name, a comment, a TODO, commented-out code, a disabled flag
     or a route that answers "not implemented".
   - Numbers, limits, roles, statuses and messages exactly as the code
     writes them. A value you did not see is a question, not a default.
   - Where the docs and the code disagree, the code is what the system
     does today; the disagreement is an open question.
   - Existing decisions are recorded, not re-decided:
     [[c]]doctrina decision new "<title>"[[/c]] citing the document they came from.

5. Acceptance criteria cite the tests that already exist. Mark one
   [[c]][verified][[/c]] only when a cited test really proves it; everything else
   stays [[c]][unverified][[/c]] — an honest gap is the point of the absorb.

6. Declare the build gate — [[c]]doctrina verify --init[[/c]] writes the commands
   the inventory found; confirm each runs what its name says — and own the
   seams the inventory lists under Interfaces (ports, env names, OpenAPI,
   protobuf, GraphQL, events) as a contract:
       [[c]]doctrina contract new <name>[[/c]]

7. Check the facts before the gates. Hand each spec, with its Source, to a
   reader that did not write it (a reviewer subagent, or a fresh session):
   for every requirement, find the code that does it; strike what cannot
   be found, and list what the code does that no requirement says.

8. Gates — fix everything they report:
       [[c]]doctrina clarify --all[[/c]]
       [[c]]doctrina validate[[/c]]
       [[c]]doctrina coverage[[/c]]

9. Show the human the Open questions, then close the absorb:
       [[c]]doctrina intake --converted[[/c]]
